Hard CapTP ideas, once each. Read in order: what a reference is, how you reach a
peer, pipelining and handoff on the wire, which dialect answers, then the memory
budget that keeps many sessions cheap. From zero on capabilities:
[capabilities](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/capabilities.md).
`Session` vs `Host` vs `Peer`: [modules]($link.page('modules')).

## A capability is a reference

Hold an unforgeable reference and you can call. Never receive it and there is
nothing to forge or guess
([CapTP](http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/),
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn),
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/)). Authority travels by
passing the reference itself, so the three moves that matter fall out for free:
delegate by handing it along, attenuate by handing a narrower wrapper, revoke by
dropping the wrapper so calls stop landing.

[A capability is a reference, not a copy. Pass it as an argument and the partner can call it; if you never receive it, there is nothing to forge.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

That picture is static: who may call whom. A call has a shape of its own. You
deliver and hold a promise at once; the partner's later resolve settles that
promise into a result.

[A single call, start to finish: you deliver it and immediately hold a promise, and the partner's resolve settles that promise to a result.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-call.svg'))

## Locator, sturdyref, swiss number

A capability is something you already hold. The first one arrives through a
**locator**: an *address*, not a reference. Spelling:
`ocapn://<designator>.<transport>`, plus optional `?key=value` **hints** for
routing. A missing hint can stop you from reaching a peer; no hint can forge one.

Two moves: open a session from the locator, then fetch a published name from the
bootstrap object. That fetch returns a live capability. After that, authority
moves by passing references.

A **sturdyref** is locator plus a **swiss number** (unguessable token naming one
object). The token survives restart; redeeming it again needs the host to have
re-registered that name under the same identity. Redeeming is the same two
moves; what returns is an ordinary reference.

| Word | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| locator | address of a peer (and how to dial it) |
| swiss number | secret name of one object at that peer |
| live reference | session authority you can call *now* |

[Everything left of the hints is an address, and none of it is authority. Only the swiss number names something you can be given.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-locator.svg'))

> **Swiss number**: holding the string *is* the permission. No list, no login.
> The name comes from the numbered Swiss bank account, where the number alone is
> the access.

Deepen:
[sturdyref diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/07-sturdyrefs-and-locators.md).
Transports: [Transports]($link.page('transports')).

## Promise pipelining

A remote call returns a **promise** immediately. You may call through that
promise *before* it resolves. The later delivers queue against the pending
answer and drain when the earlier resolve lands, so a chain of dependent calls
makes one round trip instead of one per hop.

Concrete: ask a board for a column, then that column for a card, without waiting
between asks. Chapter 06:
[pipeline and attenuate](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig).
Diagrams (the picture the code is checked against):
[promises and pipelining](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/03-promises-and-pipelining.md).

## Three-party handoff

Alice holds Carol's object and wants Bob to have it. If Bob kept calling through
Alice, Alice would stay in every later path. CapTP's handoff is a signed
introduction Bob presents to Carol himself; after that they talk directly.

Stages, in speech:

1. Alice **deposits** a gift with Carol and builds a signed **give**.
2. Alice's answer to Bob carries that give (the introduction).
3. Bob **redeems** with Carol and gets a live reference.
4. Alice is off the path.

In pancake's application surface you usually skip seeing the gift bytes: with a
Host (Zig `Bridge`, or a binding on `libpancake_driver`), a call that answers
with an introduction **settles as the introduced capability** inside the wait.
There is no separate redeem verb for ordinary app code.

Runnable: chapter 02
[introduce and redeem](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig).
In the rooms UI, _Message_ a member and the room steps out of the path
([examples]($link.page('examples'))).
Diagrams:
[handoffs](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/06-handoffs.md).

## Which dialect answers

Two peers settle CapTP **version** and **codec** from a shared **menu**: keep
what both offer, most-preferred first. Empty intersection aborts. Over a network
no menu travels on the wire; each end's offers are configuration, and both sides
compute the same pick before `op:start-session` carries the agreed version
string. Syrup (or Cap'n Proto) is a *codec*, a way to write a message down, not
the protocol itself. The session deals in messages; a separate codec layer does
the writing. Codec tradeoffs: [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')).

[Both peers run the same rule over the two menus and reach the same pick.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-negotiation.svg'))

[The dialect depends on who answers: the canonical floor against Goblins, pancake's own profile between two pancake peers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-dialects.svg'))

Against Goblins, CapTP **1.0** over Syrup is the floor: if they disagree,
pancake is wrong. Between two pancake peers the menu can reach `pancake-next`
(often Cap'n Proto). Application code stays the same; only the bytes change.

[The Spritely Goblins mascot, sizing up a stack of pancakes. pancake is checked against a real Goblins peer, so the two have to agree on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('pancake-goblin-512.png'))

## Frames: owned or borrowed

Wire bytes arrive. The engine first sees a **`FrameView`**: a borrow over those
bytes for one dispatch. When the event model must keep the message, it takes
exactly one owned **`Frame`** copy. Session tables never keep a borrow alive
across a turn. That steady inbound delivery is a checked `== 1` allocation.
Warmed core session state sits near two kilobytes. That is a design budget for
roughly half a million sessions on one node, not a live-socket RSS measurement.
[architecture](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/architecture.md),
[Performance]($link.page('performance')),
[ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md).

## Distributed GC

Export table slots carry reference counts; answer slots are single-reference
and released outright. Drop a fetched far reference (with GC enabled on the
binding) and the engine tells the peer (`op:gc-exports` / `op:gc-answers`) so
unused slots leave. Off by default; scope
is `fetch` results, not every remote name. Without GC, imports keep their rows
until teardown.
Diagrams:
[GC](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/05-gc.md).

## What proves it

Every semantic claim here is backed by something that runs:

- **Live Goblins interop.** Zig `goblins-interop` holds a real two-way OCapN
  conversation with a Spritely Goblins peer. Binding `*-goblins-interop` steps
  prove the client direction (keyed handshake, bootstrap fetch, call).
- **The upstream [OCapN test suite](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn-test-suite)**,
  run against a live pancake peer.
- **Cross-codec replay (pancake-to-pancake only).** The same conversation through
  Syrup and Cap'n Proto must produce an identical trace. That is not a Goblins
  parity claim.

Inventory of what those cover today:
[shipped](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/shipped.md).
Watch a round-trip: [examples]($link.page('examples')).

